6. Explain Spring Bean Scopes. What Is a Practical Use Case for Prototype Scope?

1. Singleton creates one bean instance per Spring application context, while prototype creates a new instance whenever Spring is asked for that bean.
2. Request scope creates one bean per HTTP request, and session scope creates one bean per HTTP session.
3. Prototype scope is useful for stateful, short-lived objects that should not share instance state between operations.
4. If a prototype bean is injected directly into a singleton, it is generally resolved only during singleton creation. To obtain a fresh instance when needed, use `ObjectProvider`.

Example:

    @Component
    @Scope(ConfigurableBeanFactory.SCOPE_PROTOTYPE)
    class ReportProcessor {
    }

    @Service
    class ReportService {
        private final ObjectProvider<ReportProcessor> provider;

        ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportProcessor> provider) {
            this.provider = provider;
        }

        ReportProcessor createProcessor() {
            return provider.getObject();
        }
    }

